WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:02.380
- [Interviewer] All right, Greg.

00:00:02.380 --> 00:00:03.690
Greg, where'd you grow up?

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:05.180
Where are you from, originally?

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:06.940
- South Central, Los Angeles.
- South Central.

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:11.131
- [Greg] Around by Hoover
Street in Vermont, in that area,

00:00:11.131 --> 00:00:12.150
back in the 70s.

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:13.970
- [Interviewer] And tell
me about your childhood.

00:00:13.970 --> 00:00:16.260
You had both your parents
when you were a kid?

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:17.270
- No, I just had my...

00:00:17.270 --> 00:00:20.010
Really, I had my mother
and my grandmother,

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:21.080
but mostly my grandmother

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:23.450
because my mother, she did...

00:00:23.450 --> 00:00:25.970
was doing a lot of time in
jail, and all that stuff,

00:00:25.970 --> 00:00:29.043
because she used to steal
and all that, you know,

00:00:29.043 --> 00:00:30.950
what they called boosting-

00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:32.160
- [Interviewer] Yeah
- back then in the days,

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:33.423
but she was a booster.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:36.710
So, basically just my grandmother.

00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:38.260
- [Interviewer] And where's dad?

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:39.610
- Hmm?
- Where's your dad?

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:42.510
- Really, I couldn't
tell you. I don't know.

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:44.000
He was like...

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:45.750
But last I heard he
was somewhere in Texas,

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:47.140
but he wasn't around then.

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:51.000
And last I seen my dad was
probably about my sixth birthday.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:52.770
My six year birthday,

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:55.070
that's probably the last time I seen him,

00:00:55.070 --> 00:00:57.307
and I aint see him one more, so...

00:00:57.307 --> 00:00:59.680
- [Interviewer] And so
how was your childhood?

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:01.250
- It was kinda rough.

00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:02.880
It was rough 'cause it..

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:04.270
Basically 'cause I grew up in a house

00:01:04.270 --> 00:01:06.310
where it was more women than anything,

00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:09.370
more women around, it wasn't
that many male figures

00:01:09.370 --> 00:01:10.203
- [Interviewer] No brothers?

00:01:10.203 --> 00:01:12.520
- No. Brothers, but he was in....

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:13.627
Well, it's just...

00:01:13.627 --> 00:01:15.460
'Cause this is part it's kind of funny

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:16.293
'cause

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:21.150
I have a different father
than my other three brothers.

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:23.290
Now, one of my three
brothers grew up with me,

00:01:23.290 --> 00:01:27.610
the other two was staying
like with their father

00:01:27.610 --> 00:01:30.340
like in Compton somewhere, right?

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:31.510
So I never even knew them.

00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:32.343
I never even met them,

00:01:32.343 --> 00:01:33.880
they never came around or anything.

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:35.840
And then the brother that lived with us,

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:40.510
he did most of his time in
Camp YAE and stuff like that.

00:01:40.510 --> 00:01:43.220
So I was basically by
myself, really, you know,

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:45.760
without the support of
like brothers and stuff.

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:48.620
I don't have no sisters, you know.

00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:53.620
And, yeah, it was kinda rough growing up.

00:01:53.757 --> 00:01:56.420
And then that's where all the influence

00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:59.430
from the guys in the neighborhood
came in from, you know,

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:01.670
not having that many males around.

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:04.330
So the influence that
I got from male figures

00:02:04.330 --> 00:02:06.230
came from the streets, you know,

00:02:06.230 --> 00:02:09.660
and guys in the streets
that was doing things,

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:12.490
that names popped in, you know,

00:02:12.490 --> 00:02:16.320
and stuff like that, you know, so...

00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:19.170
- [Interviewer] So you joined a gang?

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:20.003
- Oh yeah.

00:02:20.003 --> 00:02:20.836
- [Interviewer] Which gang? Hoover?

00:02:20.836 --> 00:02:22.630
- Hoover, yeah, yeah.

00:02:22.630 --> 00:02:26.187
I was from Hoover and, you know,

00:02:26.187 --> 00:02:29.750
and it made me feel like a
part of something, you know,

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:31.453
I was a part of something then.

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:34.220
And I was like...

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:38.030
You see I'm a small guy, so
I was always small, you know.

00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:41.920
So a lot of guys used to
test me a lot, you know,

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:42.753
'cause I was small

00:02:42.753 --> 00:02:44.653
and so I had to pretty much prove myself.

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:47.140
And once I got...

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:49.810
became a member of a gang, you know,

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:52.710
I really didn't have
that problems that much.

00:02:52.710 --> 00:02:53.543
Only problem...

00:02:53.543 --> 00:02:56.680
Time I had that problem was
with guys inside the gang,

00:02:56.680 --> 00:02:58.440
that's from my gang.

00:02:58.440 --> 00:02:59.610
But outside of us,

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:02.320
never had that problem
no more of that, really,

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:04.400
'cause they didn't want mess around.

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:06.100
They didn't want, you know, problems.

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:07.286
- [Interviewer] And
Hoover is a powerful gang

00:03:07.286 --> 00:03:08.150
- (laughs) Yeah.

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:11.890
Especially back then in
the '80s, they was bad.

00:03:11.890 --> 00:03:13.314
It was bad.

00:03:13.314 --> 00:03:14.147
- [Interviewer] What do they call them,

00:03:14.147 --> 00:03:15.430
Crip and Blood Killers?

00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:17.820
- Well, back then it was
Crips, there wasn't...

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:22.550
This, the new Crips and Blood
Killers things came later on,

00:03:22.550 --> 00:03:24.190
in like in the '90s and stuff.

00:03:24.190 --> 00:03:26.940
But I'll be 52 in July,

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:28.817
so I was just the Crips,

00:03:28.817 --> 00:03:30.870
we was all Crips, you know.

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:34.430
So it wasn't no orange color,

00:03:34.430 --> 00:03:35.310
and all this and that,

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:36.190
it was just blue,

00:03:36.190 --> 00:03:38.390
we was Crips.
- [Interviewer] Yeah. Oh.

00:03:38.390 --> 00:03:42.640
So you, at some point, got
into selling drugs, right?

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:47.100
- Yes. And that's from the
time that crack epidemic, like,

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:50.960
first kicked in, like, in around '83, '84.

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:51.980
'83, and...

00:03:53.343 --> 00:03:55.840
And, we used to be, me and a
couple of more of my buddies,

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:58.390
we used to hang on 81st,

00:03:58.390 --> 00:04:00.977
between Hoover and Vermont, which was...

00:04:00.977 --> 00:04:03.630
They just call it the tracks, right?

00:04:03.630 --> 00:04:06.570
And that's where all the
drug action was going on,

00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:07.750
where guys used to come...

00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:09.920
People used to come by drugs and stuff.

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:13.330
And there was this one apartment
building we used to hang at

00:04:13.330 --> 00:04:16.120
where most of the customers came.

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:19.830
So it was me and about three or four

00:04:19.830 --> 00:04:22.130
of my other homies, right?

00:04:22.130 --> 00:04:24.253
And we, at that time,

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:26.120
when it first came...

00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:27.900
like crack first...

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:29.940
It wasn't really...

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:33.530
Like, if you didn't use
the pipe, it was cool.

00:04:33.530 --> 00:04:36.270
If you just put some on
some weed and smoke it,

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:38.843
which what we used to do, so...

00:04:38.843 --> 00:04:39.676
- [Interviewer] Primos.

00:04:39.676 --> 00:04:40.960
- Yeah, primos, you know

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:42.400
So we used to rob...

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:44.820
We used to rob the customers.

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:47.680
We would rob some customers
and then we'll go buy us some,

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:50.010
we'll crush it all down
then make some primos,

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:52.480
and smoke and go rob customers, and rob...

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:55.510
You know, we would just
go back like that, right?

00:04:55.510 --> 00:04:58.840
Never really was trying to
hurt nobody but we was just...

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:01.109
Just we liked the primos

00:05:01.109 --> 00:05:02.010
and this was our neighborhood.

00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:04.670
and you're coming over here, you know?

00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:06.720
And a couple of guys,

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:09.300
it's guys in his building,
this apartment building,

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:11.410
used to sell dope for somebody

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:12.800
and we didn't know,

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:16.160
we hardly new that name but we
didn't know the dude, right?

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:18.200
Which was Freeway, was Rick,

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:20.520
some of Rick workers was in there.

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:23.650
So they got tired of us robbing
the customers and all that,

00:05:23.650 --> 00:05:25.193
so they went and told Rick.

00:05:26.140 --> 00:05:27.770
So one night we was all out there

00:05:27.770 --> 00:05:31.667
and some guy pulled up,
jumped out of his truck, like,

00:05:31.667 --> 00:05:33.117
"Hey, man. Let me holler at y'all man."

00:05:33.117 --> 00:05:35.510
And we looking at him like,
"Who the fuck is this dude?"

00:05:35.510 --> 00:05:36.626
I mean, can I cuss?

00:05:36.626 --> 00:05:37.659
No.
- [Interviewer] Yeah, yeah.

00:05:37.659 --> 00:05:39.977
- All right. Like, "Who the
is this dude?" you know?

00:05:39.977 --> 00:05:40.810
And he come over,

00:05:40.810 --> 00:05:44.650
"Look, man. Y'all don't
got to do that, look."

00:05:44.650 --> 00:05:47.880
And he go, he said, "Stay
here and I'll be right back."

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:49.727
So we stayed and somebody is like,

00:05:49.727 --> 00:05:50.943
"Man, that was Rick. That was Rick.

00:05:50.943 --> 00:05:52.750
That was Rick, right?"

00:05:52.750 --> 00:05:54.610
So he comes back.

00:05:54.610 --> 00:05:59.570
he comes back with like a
Ziploc baggie full of dope.

00:05:59.570 --> 00:06:02.300
So he just started passing it to us.

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:04.077
It's like four or five of us,

00:06:04.077 --> 00:06:06.417
and he pass it all out to us.

00:06:06.417 --> 00:06:08.800
And he told us that, "Just sell that.

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:11.520
Sell this and come back to me,

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:12.620
and I'll get y'all some more.

00:06:12.620 --> 00:06:13.570
And y'all don't have to do all that, man.

00:06:13.570 --> 00:06:15.620
Y'all can do what you y'all want to do."

00:06:15.620 --> 00:06:18.640
And that's like where it
started, like right there.

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:20.560
So we did it.

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:23.010
Some of us continued
to keep smoking primos,

00:06:23.010 --> 00:06:26.700
some of us seen the opportunity
of having this stuff

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:27.640
and getting to some...

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:29.980
making a lot of money, you know?

00:06:29.980 --> 00:06:33.250
So, you know, we sold some.

00:06:33.250 --> 00:06:34.833
Some of us went around there,

00:06:35.730 --> 00:06:37.310
got more, he gave us more,

00:06:37.310 --> 00:06:38.840
and this and that, "Look out for us."

00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:40.970
And sometimes we didn't
even need to have money.

00:06:40.970 --> 00:06:43.620
Like, "Man, I lost my money gambling,"

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:45.330
or, "I did this or did that."

00:06:45.330 --> 00:06:48.497
And we used to go around and
he would just give us, like,

00:06:48.497 --> 00:06:50.607
"Here," bam, you know,

00:06:50.607 --> 00:06:53.090
"Go take care of yourself, man."

00:06:53.090 --> 00:06:55.543
And that's how it all, like, all started.

00:06:56.430 --> 00:06:58.250
And we went half like...

00:06:58.250 --> 00:07:02.010
Like, four of us went
half on a car. (laughs)

00:07:02.010 --> 00:07:04.150
We all bought like a old '66 Chevy,

00:07:04.150 --> 00:07:05.890
and we all put our money in.

00:07:05.890 --> 00:07:08.700
I think we paid probably like 600,

00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:10.748
600 or 700 for it.

00:07:10.748 --> 00:07:12.920
It was a '66 Chevy, right?

00:07:12.920 --> 00:07:15.270
And we take turns driving it

00:07:15.270 --> 00:07:16.940
but we wouldn't go outside
of the neighborhood.

00:07:16.940 --> 00:07:18.130
Basically we would just ride

00:07:18.130 --> 00:07:20.020
between Florence and Manchester,

00:07:20.020 --> 00:07:21.770
and Vermont and Figueroa,

00:07:21.770 --> 00:07:24.950
that's the only area we
would ride around in, right?

00:07:24.950 --> 00:07:26.796
But we loaded, packed...

00:07:26.796 --> 00:07:30.180
Car packed with guns and
all this and that and we...

00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:31.780
just looking for stuff like that

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:36.740
Before it's the drugs...

00:07:36.740 --> 00:07:37.573
the drug game,

00:07:37.573 --> 00:07:40.780
and it was getting
really popular right then

00:07:40.780 --> 00:07:42.900
and at that time, right?

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:45.467
Now in that apartment building
that I was telling you about,

00:07:45.467 --> 00:07:49.183
it was this Jamaican lived
in there, this Jamaican guy,

00:07:49.183 --> 00:07:51.060
he used to smoke.

00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:52.930
They used to call him King Flee,

00:07:52.930 --> 00:07:54.910
he was a Jamaican named King Flee.

00:07:54.910 --> 00:07:56.680
And he used to...

00:07:56.680 --> 00:07:57.990
He used to cook dope.

00:07:57.990 --> 00:07:59.110
He used to cook dope.

00:07:59.110 --> 00:08:00.560
And he taught me

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:02.690
I used to always watch him all the time.

00:08:02.690 --> 00:08:05.330
So he basically taught
me how to cook dope,

00:08:05.330 --> 00:08:08.060
and I was only like 14, 15.

00:08:08.060 --> 00:08:09.440
- [Interviewer] Which is making me rock?

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:12.100
- Yeah, yeah. Turning the
powder into rock, right.

00:08:12.100 --> 00:08:15.887
So I used to watch him and
I learned how to do it.

00:08:15.887 --> 00:08:19.000
And at that time it wasn't
that many people who knew how,

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:20.780
but I learned how to do it real fast.

00:08:20.780 --> 00:08:23.920
So now, as far as Hoover's concerned,

00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:26.410
you got the differences sections,

00:08:26.410 --> 00:08:30.250
the 50s, the 70s, and 80s, like that.

00:08:30.250 --> 00:08:32.000
I'm from the 70s

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:34.393
but a lot of guys I used to hang with

00:08:34.393 --> 00:08:35.680
they was from Eight-Tray

00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:37.280
and I was from Seven-Four.

00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:41.157
So a lot of guys didn't know
how to cook, like I said,

00:08:41.157 --> 00:08:43.020
and I learned how to cook from Rick.

00:08:43.020 --> 00:08:45.900
I mean, not from Rick,
from King Flee, right?

00:08:45.900 --> 00:08:50.000
So a lot of guys started
coming to get me to cook dope,

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:52.053
to cook the dope up, right?

00:08:52.980 --> 00:08:55.790
So I cook it up for
them and all that stuff.

00:08:55.790 --> 00:08:57.100
And it was crazy

00:08:57.100 --> 00:08:59.530
because I don't know
how it got back to Rick,

00:08:59.530 --> 00:09:01.630
but one day I was cooking
for my homie, Bug,

00:09:01.630 --> 00:09:03.640
and he came in the house...

00:09:03.640 --> 00:09:05.810
And Bug left and came back with him

00:09:05.810 --> 00:09:07.630
and showed him like, "Look."

00:09:07.630 --> 00:09:09.160
Like, and I was cooking all...

00:09:09.160 --> 00:09:09.993
I was cooking the dope.

00:09:09.993 --> 00:09:10.826
I used to use...

00:09:10.826 --> 00:09:13.600
In my grand mother's
house, her dish drainer.

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:17.680
I used to have it filled up
with like the baby food bottles,

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:20.150
and I have them all
lined up inside of there.

00:09:20.150 --> 00:09:21.830
So he came one day and
he said, "Oh, yeah."

00:09:21.830 --> 00:09:23.020
And he seen what I was doing,

00:09:23.020 --> 00:09:25.520
he seen that I kinda
knew what I was doing,

00:09:25.520 --> 00:09:26.353
and he's asking me,

00:09:26.353 --> 00:09:27.670
"How did you just learn how to
do this, and this and that?"

00:09:27.670 --> 00:09:28.710
And I just told him and shit.

00:09:28.710 --> 00:09:29.543
And he was like,

00:09:29.543 --> 00:09:33.000
"Well, I got some stuff I
need you you to help me out."

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:34.950
So one day he came and got me, man.

00:09:34.950 --> 00:09:38.030
I mean, I probably was
cooking dope for like...

00:09:38.030 --> 00:09:40.120
It seemed like two days all day,

00:09:40.120 --> 00:09:43.127
all day just cooking, cooking,
cooking, and smoking primos.

00:09:43.127 --> 00:09:45.500
And he didn't really care, I'd just smoke

00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:46.470
That's why I stay up.

00:09:46.470 --> 00:09:47.303
I stayed up...

00:09:47.303 --> 00:09:49.220
I stay woke for weeks, weeks,

00:09:49.220 --> 00:09:50.550
not just constantly,

00:09:50.550 --> 00:09:52.440
but I might stay up for a couple of days

00:09:52.440 --> 00:09:55.780
and sleep for a few hours
and back up, you know?

00:09:56.631 --> 00:09:57.464
And I was young,

00:09:57.464 --> 00:10:00.720
I guess I had a lot of energy
or whatever, you know, really.

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:04.780
Yeah, and then he used to give us...

00:10:04.780 --> 00:10:06.270
give us shows

00:10:06.270 --> 00:10:09.360
and gives all kinds of like car shows,

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:13.330
rap concerts, and all this
different type of stuff.

00:10:13.330 --> 00:10:15.780
And he used to really look
out for our neighborhood.

00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:18.060
That's why a lot of people say

00:10:18.060 --> 00:10:19.940
Ricky dealt with the
Hoovers more than anything,

00:10:19.940 --> 00:10:22.117
he has a lot of spots
in our hood, you know,

00:10:22.117 --> 00:10:23.410
and we used to look out for him,

00:10:23.410 --> 00:10:25.567
and he looked out for us, you know?

00:10:25.567 --> 00:10:28.800
And it started, you know, like that.

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:32.960
But I guess they had a little
clique called Freeways,

00:10:32.960 --> 00:10:34.740
Freeway Boys.

00:10:34.740 --> 00:10:37.850
So I guess I was one of them, you know.

